🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗣𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You deployed an application to Kubernetes, but the pod is stuck in Pending. What will you check?”

I wouldn't immediately restart the pod.

First, I want to understand why Kubernetes cannot schedule it.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check the pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

2️⃣ Describe the pod

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

The Events section is one of the first places I would check.

Look for messages related to:

🔹 Insufficient CPU/Memory
🔹 Node selector/affinity
🔹 Taints and tolerations
🔹 Volume/PVC issues
🔹 Scheduling constraints

3️⃣ Check available nodes

kubectl get nodes

Then:

kubectl describe nodes

I would check whether nodes are Ready and whether they have enough allocatable resources.

4️⃣ Check resource requests

A pod can remain Pending when its resource requests cannot be satisfied by any available node.

I would review:

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

and check the CPU/memory requests defined in the workload.

5️⃣ Check PVC status

If the application uses persistent storage:

kubectl get pvc -n <namespace>

A PVC stuck in Pending can prevent the workload from starting correctly.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Pod → Events → Nodes → Resources → Scheduling Rules → PVC → Root Cause

Once the actual cause is identified, I would apply the appropriate fix and verify:

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -w

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Pending doesn't mean the application is broken.

It often means Kubernetes hasn't been able to schedule the pod yet.
